### Summary

`surrealdb-parser` (workspace crate `surrealdb/parser` in `surrealdb/surrealdb`) is the in-repo next-generation SurrealQL parser. Its lexer wrapper `PeekableLexer<'source, 4>` keeps lookahead tokens in `[MaybeUninit<Result<Token, LexError>>; 4]` with two `u8` cursors, and `Parser::peek1()`/`peek2()` expose one- and two-token lookahead through the public, safe `Parse` trait (`async fn parse(parser: &mut Parser<'_, '_>) -> ParseResult<Self>`). In `surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs:88-103` the loop that is meant to lex forward to the requested offset is inverted: the inclusive range `write..=read` is empty whenever the target is ahead of the buffered count, so any forward jump (`OFFSET > buffered`) skips lexing and then executes `self.peek[read as usize].assume_init()` on a slot that was never written. Because `Token` contains the fieldless enum `BaseTokenKind`, constructing `Result<Token, LexError>` from uninitialized bytes reads an uninitialized enum discriminant - undefined behavior. Miri confirms it at `surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs:103:17`: `constructing invalid value of type std::result::Result<surrealdb_token::Token, surrealdb_token::LexError>: at .<enum-tag>, encountered uninitialized memory, but expected an integer`. A second, deterministic consequence (no UB required) is that consumed ring slots are never cleared, so `peek1()` can return a stale, already-consumed token; the inlined integration test shows this under a plain `cargo test` (it returns the token at span `0..1` after the three-token input `"a b c"` has been fully consumed; the correct result is `None`). No `unsafe` is needed at the call site: any crate implementing the public, unsealed `Parse` trait receives a `&mut Parser` and can call `peek1()`/`peek2()` before peeking the current token (or skip an offset), then run `Parser::enter_parse::<P>(...)`.

### Details

**Verified snapshot.** `surrealdb 3.3.0-nightly`, git commit `18971ffba781a796c0ad3897bb4b82432c241e4c` (2026-09-04); file `surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs` (166 lines), package `surrealdb-parser` (`surrealdb/parser/Cargo.toml:2`), workspace member at `Cargo.toml:13`. The crate is workspace-internal: `publish.workspace = true` and the workspace sets `publish = false` (`Cargo.toml:35`), so it is not on crates.io; the workspace alias is declared at `Cargo.toml:60`. Upstream `surrealdb/surrealdb` `main` carries the identical `peek::<OFFSET>` body (verified 2026-09-21), so this is not a fork-local defect. We verified only this snapshot; please set the final affected/patched ranges when publishing.

**Root cause.** `PeekableLexer` (`peekable.rs:8-13`) initialises every slot with `MaybeUninit::uninit()` (`:37`). `peek::<OFFSET>` (`:64-105`) handles `OFFSET == 0` by lexing when the buffer is empty; for `OFFSET > 0` it computes the target ring index and then tries to fill up to it:

```rust
// surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs:87-104 (as verified)
} else {
    let write = self.write & Self::MASK;
    let read = self.read.wrapping_add(OFFSET) & Self::MASK;

    let write = if write < read {
        write + SIZE as u8
    } else {
        write
    };

    for _ in write..=read {
        let x = self.lex_token()?;
        self.peek[(self.write & Self::MASK) as usize] = MaybeUninit::new(x);
        self.write = self.write.wrapping_add(1);
    }

    Some(unsafe { self.peek[read as usize].assume_init() })
}
```

The range adjustment is backwards. When the target is ahead of the write cursor in ring order (`write < read`), `write` is pushed past `read` by `SIZE`, so the inclusive range `write..=read` is empty; when the target is behind (`write > read`), the range is also empty; only `write == read` runs the body, lexing exactly one token. The only case that fills anything is therefore "the requested offset is exactly the next unbuffered position", i.e. the sequential `peek()` → `peek1()` → `peek2()` pattern used by the crate's own call sites. The intent was clearly to fill from the buffered count up to the offset - the production parser has exactly that loop in its safe path: `surrealdb/core/src/syn/parser/mod.rs:297-303` (`peek_token_at`), `for _ in self.token_buffer.len()..=at`, over a `[Token; S]` buffer initialised with `Token::invalid()` (`surrealdb/core/src/syn/parser/token_buffer.rs:4-76`). Two consequences follow: (1) any forward jump leaves the target slot unwritten and `assume_init()` reads uninitialized memory (UB); (2) because `next()`/`pop_peek()`/`peek::<0>` never clear slots, a target index can also hit an old, already-consumed token, which is returned as a fresh token (a deterministic parser desynchronisation, no UB).

**Why safe code can reach it.** `Parse` is public and unsealed (`parse/mod.rs:66-70`); `Parser::enter_parse` / `enter_parse_reuse` / `enter_partial_parse` are `pub` (`:219`, `:233`, `:293`) and `Parser::peek1` / `peek2` are `pub` (`:531-556`). Implementing `Parse` for a local type and calling `parser.peek1()` (or `peek2()`) as the first lookahead is ordinary, safe Rust; the uninitialized read happens inside `PeekableLexer::peek`. The crate's own 31 call sites of `peek1`/`peek2`/`peek_joined1`/`peek_joined2` (`parse/expr.rs` 9, `parse/prime.rs` 10, `parse/stmt/mod.rs` 4, `parse/range.rs` 4, `parse/record_id.rs` 2, `parse/mod.rs` 2) are all inside branches that already peeked the current token (or are guarded by a successful `peek_joined1`), so the invariant `OFFSET <= buffered` currently holds by convention only. It is neither expressed in the type, nor asserted, nor documented; the only precondition assertion in this area is `Parser::lex`'s `assert!(!self.lex.has_peek(), ...)` (`parse/mod.rs:786`), showing that buffer-state preconditions were considered for one method but not for `peek`.

**Reproduction.** The inlined integration test (`surrealdb/parser/tests/f3_parser_peekable_uninit_ub.rs`, same source as the collapsible block) runs four probes through `Parser::enter_parse`: three `#[ignore]`d UB probes (empty-buffer `peek1()`, empty-buffer `peek2()`, `peek()` then `peek2()`) and one deterministic stale-slot probe, plus a sequential-lookahead control that must pass. On the reporter's host (Linux x86_64, `cargo +nightly-2026-04-14`, Miri component installed): `cargo test ... -- --nocapture` → `1 passed; 0 failed; 4 ignored` (control only); `cargo test ... -- --ignored ... stale_slot_is_returned_as_a_new_token` → **FAILED**, printing `Some(Token { token: Ident, joined: Joined, span: Span { start: 0, end: 1 } })` (the already-consumed first token) where the correct value is `None`; `cargo miri test ... -- --ignored ... peek1_on_fresh_parser_reads_never_written_slot` → **UB reported at `surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs:103:17`** with the message quoted above. Raw outputs are inlined below (paths sanitised; content otherwise verbatim).

**Async specificity (for context).** `Parse::parse` is an `async fn`, and `Parser::enter_parse_reuse` drives it through `reblessive::Stack::enter(...).step()` (`parse/mod.rs:267-289`), so the parser future - carrying the `PeekableLexer` lookahead state across suspension points - is type-erased into a heap stack. The Miri backtrace runs `Parse::parse` → `Parser::parse` (`:754`) → `reblessive::Stack::enter_future` → `Runner::step` → `enter_parse_reuse` (`:270`) → `enter_parse` (`:228`). The defect itself is not cancellation-specific, but it is a soundness hole in the lookahead state of the asynchronous parser, of the familiar `unsafe`-free "`MaybeUninit` slot + `assume_init`" class.

**Honest scope.** We did not find an in-tree call site or production path that triggers the uninitialized read today, and the crate currently has no production consumers: the shipped query path parses with `surrealdb/core/src/syn`, whose `TokenBuffer<4>` uses the safe `[Token; S]` + `Token::invalid()` design and correctly fills forward (`core/src/syn/parser/mod.rs:297-303`). The crate is nevertheless in-tree, has a fuzz target (`surrealdb/parser/fuzz/fuzz_targets/fuzz_parser.rs`), a benchmark and examples, and is the designated replacement parser; the fuzz target only drives the built-in `Query` production, which peeks before it looks ahead, so fuzzing does not cover this public-API shape. No remote/black-box vector is claimed. This is a public-safe-API soundness hole: any new in-tree `Parse` implementation (or downstream user, once the crate is published) that performs a forward-jump lookahead makes it reachable, and once the parser is wired into the query path the input is untrusted query text.

**Suggested remediation.** Fix the fill loop (e.g. lex from the buffered count up to `OFFSET`: `for _ in buffered..=OFFSET`), or port the production parser's safe design (`[Token; S]`, `Token::invalid()`, bounds-checked `at()`). If sequential lookahead really is the intended contract, express it as a `debug_assert!`/documented precondition instead of silently reading a slot. The stale-slot probe should be added to the test suite as a regression test (it fails before the fix and passes after); the Miri probes exercise the UB variant. None of this touches public signatures.

**Disclosure.** Not disclosed publicly or to any third party. The complete PoC source and the raw `cargo test`/Miri outputs are inlined below; the full research report is available on request.

### PoC

Minimal trigger (safe code only):

```rust
use surrealdb_parser::parse::ParseResult;
use surrealdb_parser::{Config, Parse, Parser};

struct Probe;
impl Parse for Probe {
    async fn parse(parser: &mut Parser<'_, '_>) -> ParseResult<Self> {
        // Fresh parser: lookahead buffer is empty (read == write == 0).
        let _ = parser.peek1()?; // peek::<1> reads never-written slot 1 -> UB
        Ok(Probe)
    }
}

let _ = Parser::enter_parse::<Probe>("a b c", Config::default());
```

Run (copy the inlined integration-test source to `surrealdb/parser/tests/` first; `tests/*.rs` is auto-discovered):

```bash
# control + stale-slot probe
cargo +nightly-2026-04-14 test -p surrealdb-parser --test f3_parser_peekable_uninit_ub -- --nocapture
cargo +nightly-2026-04-14 test -p surrealdb-parser --test f3_parser_peekable_uninit_ub -- \
  --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1 stale_slot_is_returned_as_a_new_token

# UB oracle
rustup +nightly-2026-04-14 component add miri
cargo +nightly-2026-04-14 miri test -p surrealdb-parser --test f3_parser_peekable_uninit_ub -- \
  --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1 peek1_on_fresh_parser_reads_never_written_slot
```

Observed (verbatim excerpts; full outputs in the collapsible blocks below):

```text
